%S A401705 1,2,3,4,5,3,7,8,9,5,11,3,13,7,5,16,17,9,19,5,21,11,23,3,25,13,27,7,
%T A401705 29,5,31,32,11,17,35,36,37,19,39,5,41,21,43,11,5,23,47,3,49,50,17,13,
%U A401705 53,27,55,7,57,29,59,5,61,31,63,64,65,11,67,17,23,35,71,9,73,37,75,19,77,39,79,5,81,41,83,21,85,43
%N A401705 a(n) is a unitary divisor d of n with gcd(d, sigma(d)) = 1 constructed by the algorithm B in Pollack & Pomerance "Prime-Perfect Numbers" paper. See comments for the description.
%C A401705 Algorithm works by multiplying together all such prime divisors p^e || n for which the cumulative product still remains a term of A014567, starting from the higher end (A053585) of the prime divisors of n, and iterating towards the least (A028233) prime divisor of n. Note that the result is not always equal to A401707(n), the largest unitary divisor d of n with gcd(d, sigma(d)) = 1, from which this indeed differs at n=12, 24, 40, 45, 48, etc. See A401704.
%H A401705 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A401705/b401705.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401705 Paul Pollack and Carl Pomerance, <a href="http://www.integers-ejcnt.org/a14self/a14self.Abstract.html">Prime-Perfect Numbers</a>, INTEGERS, Electronic J. of Combinatorial Number Theory, Vol. 12a, Paper A14, 2012. (See page 6).
%H A401705 <a href="/index/Si#SIGMAN">Index entries for sequences related to sigma(n)</a>.
%F A401705 a(n) <= A401707(n).
%e A401705 For n = 12 = 2^2 * 3^1, we start from the prime divisor 3, which satisfies our condition as sigma(3) [= 4] is coprime with 3, so the first partial product is 3. Then at the next step we consider the prime factor 2^2, but sigma(2^2 * 3) = 28, which is not coprime with 2^2 * 3, so we cannot include 2^2 in the product, which thus remains at 3, and therefore a(12) = 3.
%e A401705 For n = 42 = 2 * 3 * 7, we start from the prime divisor 7, which satisfies our condition as sigma(7) = 8 is coprime with 7, so the first partial product is 7. Then we consider the next highest prime factor, 3, and now sigma(3*7) = 32, which is coprime with 21, so we include it in the product. Finally we consider the prime factor 2, and now sigma(2*3*7) = 96 is not coprime with 42, so we don't use that 2, and the result remains as 3*7, therefore a(42) = 21.
%e A401705 For n=90 = 2 * 3^2 * 5, we start from the prime divisor 5, which satisfies our condition as sigma(5) = 6 is coprime with 5, so the first partial product is 5. Then at the next step we consider the prime factor 3^2, but sigma(3^2 * 5) = 78 = 2*3*13, which is not coprime with 3^2 * 5, so we cannot include 3^3 in product. Then at last step we consider prime divisor 2 of 90, but also here sigma(2*5) = 18 = 2 * 3^2 is not coprime with 2*5, so the product must remain at 5, and therefore a(90) = 5.
%o A401705 (PARI) A401705(n) = { my(f=factor(n),d=1,u); forstep(i=#f~,1,-1,u = (f[i,1]^f[i,2])*d; if(1==gcd(sigma(u),u), d = u)); (d); };
%Y A401705 Cf. A000203, A014567, A028233, A053585, A401704 (positions where differs from A401707), A401706 [= n/a(n)], A401707.
%Y A401705 Cf. also A027598, A105402, A175200.
%K A401705 nonn,new
%O A401705 1,2
%A A401705 _Antti Karttunen_, Nov 03 2026
